            <h2 class="comments-header">2 Comments</h2>
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-67" class="comment">
                <p><strong><a title="https://pitchersandpoets.com" href="https://pitchersandpoets.com" rel="nofollow">Ted</a>, <a href="https://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/pujols_through_the_ages#comment-67" title="Comment permalink">Sep  9, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>It's true, Pujols is the current embodiment of the joys of the present. His consistency is almost enough to convince us, irrationally, that it'll last forever.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-68" class="comment">
                <p><strong>Jim Flint, <a href="https://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/pujols_through_the_ages#comment-68" title="Comment permalink">Sep  9, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>I quite enjoyed that Mike.  Albert is my fave...I drafted him as a rookie in the ORBL and have enjoyed the fear he instills in my replay opponents.  I have passed on many insane trade offers for him over the years.  One trade that still sticks in my mind is an offer for an entire starting pitching staff(5 good pitchers mostly in their prime years) after a pause to consider(about 15 seconds)I thought that I would still rather have Albert.
  My Dad..well he's still kickin it old school.  We had a party for his 80th birthday this past July.  Sometimes we couldn't talk either.  We could always talk sports though.  Hockey of course(hey we're Canadian eh!!),Baseball and Football(mostly Canadian)we both like the Canadian version better than the NFL.  Music too.  I guess both our fave things are sports and music and the apple didn't fall far from the tree.  </p>

<p>I should call my Dad tomorrow.</p>

